id,word,part_of_speech,cefr_level,definition,example_sentence,notification_line_1,notification_line_2,example_source,context_scenario,context_source,story_text,story_source,context_enabled
1,aberration,noun,C1,departure from what is normal,The sudden heatwave was an aberration in spring.,Noted an aberration in the data,You noticed the deviation,LexaWall Educational Content,"Scientists studying climate patterns discovered an unusual aberration in seasonal trends last summer. The temperature spike was completely unexpected and didn't match historical data spanning decades. Researchers puzzled over the anomaly for months, collecting data from multiple sources. Eventually, they traced the cause to a combination of atmospheric conditions rarely seen together. The aberration prompted new climate models to better account for extreme weather events.",LexaWall Educational Content,"Eva heard meteorologists discussing unusual weather patterns. She realized the sudden heatwave was truly an aberration, a rare event in the history of her region. Her understanding of climate science deepened that day.",LexaWall Educational Content,1
2,abjure,verb,C1,formally renounce a belief or claim,He chose to abjure his earlier political vows.,She publicly abjured her previous stance,You changed your stated position,LexaWall Educational Content,"During a documentary about religious reform, historians discussed leaders who publicly abjured their former beliefs. These individuals faced significant social pressure but persisted in their new convictions. Their courage inspired reforms that lasted centuries. Modern scholars study these moments as turning points in history. Such abjurations often required years of deliberation before being announced.",LexaWall Educational Content,"Marcus stood before his old community and formally abjured the political views he once held dear. The decision had taken months of reflection, but he felt compelled to speak his truth. Though some rejected him, others recognized his courage.",LexaWall Educational Content,1
3,abstruse,adjective,C1,difficult to understand because obscure,Her lecture explored abstruse philosophical problems.,Abstruse ideas dominated the evening discussion,You found it challenging to follow,LexaWall Educational Content,"The philosophy professor's new course covered abstruse concepts from medieval texts. Students found themselves rereading passages multiple times to grasp the meaning. Discussion groups formed to wrestle with the complex ideas together. Over the semester, patterns emerged and connections became clearer. By the end, what seemed abstruse initially now felt intellectually rewarding and elegant.",LexaWall Educational Content,"Kenji stared at the abstruse mathematical proof in his textbook, feeling utterly lost. He decided to watch videos and work through simpler examples first. After weeks of effort, the elegant logic finally clicked into place.",LexaWall Educational Content,1
4,acquiesce,verb,C1,accept reluctantly but without protest,They acquiesced to the committee's modest demands.,He quietly acquiesced to management's request,You did not oppose it,LexaWall Educational Content,"During the merger negotiation, the smaller company's executives debated the terms intensely. After three rounds of discussions, they ultimately decided to acquiesce to most of the buyer's demands. While not ideal, they recognized the benefits of the partnership. Their willingness to acquiesce, rather than resist indefinitely, saved time and resources. The deal closed successfully within months.",LexaWall Educational Content,"Sarah reluctantly agreed to acquiesce to her parents' suggestion about her career choice. Though she had reservations, she valued family harmony more than asserting total independence. Over time, she discovered genuine interest in the path they had recommended.",LexaWall Educational Content,1
5,acumen,noun,C1,keen insight and good judgment,Her financial acumen saved the company millions.,His acumen guided the investment strategy,You judged the situation well,LexaWall Educational Content,"The young entrepreneur's financial acumen impressed investors from day one. Her ability to spot market trends and manage resources efficiently set her apart. Within five years, she had built a profitable company from zero revenue. Colleagues credited her keen judgment and strategic insight. Her acumen was now sought after by startups across the industry.",LexaWall Educational Content,"Julia's business acumen saved the failing restaurant when she took over management. She analyzed costs, redesigned the menu, and improved customer service systematically. Within a year, the restaurant had become profitable and respected in the community.",LexaWall Educational Content,1
6,admonish,verb,C1,advise or reprimand gently,The editor admonished the writer about accuracy.,She admonished him for loose citations,You were corrected calmly,LexaWall Educational Content,"The experienced editor took time to admonish the young journalist about fact-checking practices. Rather than being harsh, she offered constructive guidance and examples of errors she'd made early in her career. The journalist appreciated the respectful approach to correction. Over time, the writer's accuracy improved noticeably. The admonishment became a turning point in the journalist's professional development.",LexaWall Educational Content,"The coach admonished his player gently for missing practice, reminding him why discipline mattered. Rather than anger, he showed care and concern for the athlete's future. The player understood the message and commitment deepened.",LexaWall Educational Content,1
7,aesthetic,adjective,C1,concerned with beauty or appearance,The building's aesthetic appealed to international critics.,Aesthetic choices shaped the gallery's display,You appreciated the visual design,LexaWall Educational Content,The architect's aesthetic philosophy emphasized natural materials and minimalist design. Museum visitors often paused to appreciate how form and function blended seamlessly. Her buildings became destinations for design enthusiasts worldwide. The aesthetic choices influenced an entire generation of architects. Her work demonstrated that beauty and practicality could coexist.,LexaWall Educational Content,"The gallery curator selected pieces with a particular aesthetic in mind: nature-inspired and vibrant. Visitors felt energized walking through the exhibition, surrounded by color and life. The curator's vision had transformed the gallery's reputation.",LexaWall Educational Content,1
8,alacrity,noun,C1,cheerful readiness or eagerness,She accepted the invitation with surprising alacrity.,He completed the task with alacrity,You responded quickly and willingly,LexaWall Educational Content,"When the volunteer opportunity was announced at the community center, participants approached with surprising alacrity. Dozens of hands shot up immediately, eager to help organize the charity event. The enthusiasm energized the planning committee from the start. Coordinators marveled at how quickly work progressed with such willing participants. The project finished two weeks ahead of schedule thanks to this alacrity.",LexaWall Educational Content,"Antonio accepted the challenge with alacrity, excited to prove himself on the new team. His eagerness to learn and contribute impressed the seasoned staff members. Within months, he had become an integral part of the group.",LexaWall Educational Content,1
9,ambivalent,adjective,C1,having mixed or conflicting feelings,She felt ambivalent about accepting promotion.,Ambivalence complicated her decision-making,You felt two ways,LexaWall Educational Content,"She felt deeply ambivalent about the job offer: the position was prestigious but required relocating away from family. For weeks, she weighed the career advancement against the relational cost. Her friends offered perspectives from both sides, understanding her conflicting emotions. Late-night conversations with her partner revealed their own mixed feelings. Finally, they made a decision together that honored both ambitions and connections.",LexaWall Educational Content,David felt ambivalent about his promotion: proud of the achievement but worried about new responsibilities. He talked with mentors who had faced similar transitions. Their reassurance helped him embrace the opportunity with greater confidence.,LexaWall Educational Content,1
10,ameliorate,verb,C1,make a situation better or less bad,Policy changes aim to ameliorate housing shortages.,Measures began to ameliorate living conditions,You observed modest improvements,LexaWall Educational Content,"City planners implemented several policies aimed to ameliorate housing shortages in low-income neighborhoods. They approved new affordable housing projects and streamlined construction permits. Community organizations partnered with the city to support residents during the transition. Over three years, conditions gradually began to ameliorate as supply increased. Families that had struggled to find affordable homes now had more options available.",LexaWall Educational Content,"The new medication helped ameliorate her chronic pain significantly. Though not a complete cure, the relief allowed her to return to activities she'd missed. Her quality of life improved dramatically with this treatment.",LexaWall Educational Content,1
11,anomaly,noun,C1,something that deviates from the norm,The scientist noted an anomaly in the data.,An anomaly disrupted the predictable pattern,You spotted the irregularity,LexaWall Educational Content,"During the astronomical survey, researchers detected an anomaly in the star's behavior that scientists couldn't immediately explain. The data showed irregularities that contradicted established models. Teams from multiple institutions collaborated to investigate the mysterious reading. After months of analysis, they discovered a previously unknown stellar phenomenon. The anomaly became evidence supporting a new theory in astrophysics.",LexaWall Educational Content,"The quality control inspector noticed an anomaly in the production batch. Rather than ignoring it, she investigated thoroughly and found a mechanical problem. Her attention to the anomaly prevented thousands of defective units from reaching customers.",LexaWall Educational Content,1
12,ascertain,verb,C1,find out with certainty,We must ascertain the facts before publishing.,Reporters worked to ascertain the source,You verified the details,LexaWall Educational Content,"Investigators worked carefully to ascertain the facts of the case before making any accusations. They interviewed witnesses, examined evidence, and reviewed documents methodically. Only after confirming details from multiple sources did they proceed. The rigorous approach to ascertain the truth ensured justice was served accurately. The defendant's innocence was eventually established through this thorough process.",LexaWall Educational Content,"Before publishing the article, the journalist made several phone calls to ascertain the facts. She wanted absolute certainty about the controversial claims. Only after verification did she feel confident submitting her story.",LexaWall Educational Content,1
13,asperity,noun,C1,severe or harsh quality of tone,His reply carried notable asperity and distance.,Asperity crept into the once gentle conversation,You sensed the harshness,LexaWall Educational Content,"The customer service representative noticed asperity creeping into the conversation as frustration built. She recognized the harsh tone and paused to reset the interaction with empathy. By acknowledging the customer's difficulty, she softened the asperity in their voice. Both parties shifted to a more collaborative problem-solving approach. The conversation ended positively despite the initial tension.",LexaWall Educational Content,The manager's asperity when speaking about missed deadlines shocked the normally gentle team. She realized her sharp tone had hurt morale and took time to apologize. She committed to addressing problems with constructive feedback rather than harshness.,LexaWall Educational Content,1
14,assuage,verb,C1,lessen intensity of an unpleasant feeling,She tried to assuage their mounting anxieties.,Warm words helped assuage his fears,You felt somewhat reassured,LexaWall Educational Content,"Parents gathered at the school to discuss their mounting concerns about curriculum changes. The principal took time to assuage their worries with detailed explanations and research data. She explained the reasoning behind the modifications and addressed specific concerns directly. Hearing her thoughtful responses helped assuage the anxiety many felt. By the end of the meeting, most parents felt reassured about the direction.",LexaWall Educational Content,The therapist helped assuage her client's fears about the upcoming surgery with evidence and honest discussion. Knowing what to expect helped ease the anxiety considerably. The patient felt more prepared and less afraid.,LexaWall Educational Content,1
15,audacious,adjective,B2,willing to take bold risks,An audacious proposal challenged the committee,You admired the boldness,LexaWall Educational Content,,"The startup's audacious plan to revolutionize renewable energy seemed impossible to traditional investors. Yet the young entrepreneurs had done their research and believed deeply in their vision. Small funding rounds supported initial prototypes that showed promise. Five years later, their audacious bet had positioned them as industry leaders. Competitors now rushed to adopt similar approaches.",LexaWall Educational Content,The young artist's audacious portfolio challenged every convention in her field. Galleries that usually rejected experimental work took notice of her bold vision. Her willingness to take risks paid off as her career flourished.,LexaWall Educational Content,1
